From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jon Pretty <jon.pretty@propensive.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 09:33:56 +0200
Subject: Do not constrain type arguments by an AnyKind bound's capture set
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`recheckTypeArg` propagates a formal type parameter's bound capture set onto the
argument, so `mkAbs[Ref^{r6}]` is rejected when the parameter is declared
`[A <: Any^{}]`. The guard exempts bounds that say nothing about captures —
`Any` and `Singleton` — but not `AnyKind`, whose capture set is empty for the
same reason `Any`'s is: it is the top of the kind lattice, not a statement about
capabilities.

The consequence is that any capturing type argument to an `AnyKind`-bounded
parameter is checked against `{}` and rejected:

    def anyKindBound[T <: AnyKind]: Unit = ()
    anyKindBound[Int => Int]   // error: Reference `any` is not included in
                               // the allowed capture set {} of the type
                               // parameter bound scala.AnyKind
    def anyBound[T]: Unit = ()
    anyBound[Int => Int]       // fine

`quoted.Type` and `TypeRepr.of` are declared `[T <: AnyKind]`, so this breaks
every capture-checked macro that mentions a function type reflectively —
`TypeRepr.of[value => value2]` and friends. Soundness's `mercator` hits it four
times building a `MethodType` for `map`.

Adding `AnyKind` to the exemptions restores that. The bound `Any^{}` still
constrains as intended: `tests/neg-custom-args/captures/capset-bounds.scala`,
added by the commit that introduced this check, still reports both of its
expected errors unchanged.

Introduced upstream in 196eada95d ("Propagate capsets of type parameter bounds
into argument capsets", 2026-07-14), which landed with the note "One failing
test, to be checked". Affects 3.10 only; 3.9 predates the check.

Developed with LLM assistance (Claude, Anthropic). Verified by building the
compiler, reducing the failure to three lines, confirming the neg test that
introduced the check is unaffected, and rebuilding Soundness's mercator module.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Proscala-Patch: anykindcap
---
 .../src/dotty/tools/dotc/cc/CheckCaptures.scala | 10 +++++++---
 .../captures/anykind-capset.scala               | 17 +++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 24 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)
 create mode 100644 tests/pos-custom-args/captures/anykind-capset.scala

diff --git a/compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/cc/CheckCaptures.scala b/compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/cc/CheckCaptures.scala
index 0cfeb6d3485d87f167ee3009569ebd4c26aa07c4..eb853450a595628c19befb175bc568be473bff58 100644
--- a/compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/cc/CheckCaptures.scala
+++ b/compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/cc/CheckCaptures.scala
@@ -1071,6 +1071,8 @@ class CheckCaptures extends Recheck, SymTransformer:
     /** Check that capture set of type argument subcaptures capture set of bounds.
      *  We don't check if
      *   - the bound is exactly any since that is capture polymorphic top, or
+     *   - the bound is AnyKind, which is the top of the kind lattice and so says
+     *     nothing about captures, exactly as Any says nothing, or
      *   - the bound is FromJavaObject (the `Object` bound of Java type parameters),
      *     which is the capture polymorphic top for Java interop, or
      *   - the bound is singleton, since that's not a "real" bound, or
@@ -1084,9 +1086,11 @@ class CheckCaptures extends Recheck, SymTransformer:
       val argRefs = argType.captureSet
       val hiBound = formal.bounds.hi
       val boundRefs = hiBound.captureSet
-      // Is `hiBound` exactly `Any`, or `FromJavaObject` (Java's `Object` bound)?
-      // These are capture polymorphic top types, so they do not constrain arguments.
-      val isPolymorphicTop = hiBound.isExactlyAny || hiBound.isFromJavaObject
+      // Is `hiBound` exactly `Any`, `AnyKind`, or `FromJavaObject` (Java's `Object`
+      // bound)? These are capture polymorphic top types, so they do not constrain
+      // arguments.
+      val isPolymorphicTop =
+        hiBound.isExactlyAny || hiBound.isRef(defn.AnyKindClass) || hiBound.isFromJavaObject
       val canCheck =
         !isPolymorphicTop && !hiBound.isRef(defn.SingletonClass)
         && !boundRefs.elems.exists:
diff --git a/tests/pos-custom-args/captures/anykind-capset.scala b/tests/pos-custom-args/captures/anykind-capset.scala
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..ec38fea85a14013cd9b144fa09a09b600b08ae95
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tests/pos-custom-args/captures/anykind-capset.scala
@@ -0,0 +1,17 @@
+import language.experimental.captureChecking
+
+// An `AnyKind`-bounded type parameter must not constrain its argument's capture
+// set: `AnyKind` is the top of the kind lattice, so it says nothing about
+// captures, exactly as `Any` says nothing. `Type`/`TypeRepr.of` are declared
+// this way, so without this a capture-checked macro cannot mention a function
+// type reflectively.
+object Test:
+  def anyKindBound[T <: AnyKind]: Unit = ()
+  def anyBound[T]: Unit = ()
+
+  def impureViaAnyKind = anyKindBound[Int => Int]
+  def impureViaAny = anyBound[Int => Int]
+
+  def reflected(using q: quoted.Quotes) =
+    import q.reflect.*
+    TypeRepr.of[Int => Int]
